-## Global constraints
-
-- The GDD wins over every older spec (AGENTS.md). Anything it marks Open, Proposal or TBD is built only as the owner's answer. **No PR in this plan decides an open D-row or an open sensitive question.** Every question that touches civilians, residents, depopulation, deportation, occupation or annexation is an **owner pick**: it lists the options with one neutral line of context, and carries no recommendation. Only technical questions (data structures, plumbing, test strategy) carry a recommendation.
-- **Sourcing.** No target, date, population figure or card text is invented. A figure from `localities_1948.yaml` (VS 1945, draft) keeps its draft status until it is checked. One value per fact, chosen by CONTENT_POLICY §1. AI-assisted prose is a draft, and a human checks every claim before `fact-checked` (CONTENT_POLICY §2.6).
-- **Balance values** (costs, X, penalties) enter `content/` as labelled design values, each set by the owner (Q-CUT-1; Q-GEN-1 below). §4.1 item 2 calls Deport's costs "large", and their numbers are the owner's.
-- **Determinism** (docs/determinism.md): one RNG, no trigonometry, no unordered iteration into state, invariant culture. **No PR in this phase adds an RNG draw.** Counts and timers are integers (spec §2.2). Settlements iterate in ordinal id order.
-- **Goldens.** Phase 6 runs after the cutover. Each PR that changes an MVP rule re-pins every golden it moves, in the same PR, with a rebaseline-history row and the reason in the commit and the PR (spec §3, "After cutover"). Golden-moving PRs **merge one at a time**. New goldens: `mvp-occupy-cost` and `mvp-deport-cost`, paired from the same state (spec §3). A scenario that issues `ResolveCapture(..., deport)` is a test of the cost. It is scenario test code, not the reference player's policy, which never chooses Deport (§4.1 item 1).
-- **Rule IDs.** Every new rule gets an `R-` ID in `docs/rules/mvp.md`, defined in the code PR together with its citing test (`rule_ids.py`). The IDs below are **proposed**. The area names need the maintainer's sign-off: R-CTRL (as #532 proposed) and R-GATE. R-EFX continues Phase 2's. Rewording a rule keeps its ID, and a retired ID is not reused.
-- **Each PR:** a branch from current `origin/main` in `.worktrees/<branch>`; a claimed issue (Task form); a `CHANGELOG.md` line for `sim/`, `content/` or `unity/`; the session's `Co-Authored-By` line on commits and its attribution line at the end of the PR body; "Refs #386" (never "Fixes" until the phase's last PR).
-- Full checks before every PR (exact commands in the Phase 1 plan, Global constraints): `dotnet test sim/Statecraft.sln` (0 warnings), the tools and policy pytest runs, `check_repo.py`, `changelog_check.py --base origin/main`, `gdd_lint.py`, `rule_ids.py` and `md_links.py`.
-- **Unity:** when `unity/` changes or the DLL's behaviour changes, run `tools/build_sim_for_unity.sh`, then EditMode under `machine_lock.py` (the Phase 1 plan's command; never `-nographics` on the editor; revert the settings churn). Every sim PR that moves a golden also needs the WebGL `web_check`. A PR that cannot run Unity says so, and it does not merge until someone with Unity has run it (CI does not run Unity).
-- **The sensitivity-review gate, per PR.** Each PR below names what the owner reviews before it merges and what the release gate enforces before a shareable build. A PR whose gate is not passed may merge only if everything it adds is either refused by the release gate or not reachable in a shareable build. The PR body says which.
